    <body>
      <sec id="article-29450.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Staphylococcal pneumonia is an uncommon but highly morbid cause of pneumonia,&#x000a0;caused by&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>, which colonizes up to one-third of individuals and can produce virulence factors and be methicillin-resistant (MRSA), complicating&#x000a0;treatment. Although&#x000a0;staphylococcal pneumonia accounts for only 1% to 3% of community-acquired pneumonia cases, this condition is a significant cause of hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia and is often associated with recent viral infections, particularly influenza. This activity reviews the risk factors, recommended diagnostic studies, and appropriate empiric and targeted therapies for staphylococcal pneumonia, including MRSA coverage and de-escalation, as well as its complications, which can rapidly progress to necrotizing pneumonia, bacteremia, or sepsis. Participants will also gain an understanding of antimicrobial stewardship and selection guided by factors such as renal function, potential drug interactions, local resistance patterns, medication availability, and cost.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance&#x000a0;learners' competence in identifying staphylococcal pneumonia, performing the recommended evaluation, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach to managing this condition,&#x000a0;thereby optimizing outcomes and reducing&#x000a0;complications in patients with this potentially life-threatening infection.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify&#x000a0;the typical presentation of a patient with staphylococcal pneumonia.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate methicillin-resistant from methicillin-sensitive <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic> infections to guide appropriate antibiotic stewardship.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select empiric antimicrobial therapy for&#x000a0;staphylococcal pneumonia based on guideline recommendations.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interprofessional team members to improve care coordination and outcomes in patients with staphylococcal pneumonia.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=29450&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=29450">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;(<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>) colonizes 15% to 33% of the human population, with a predilection for certain sites, including the intestine, nares, and skin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r1">[1]</xref>
<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> is known to cause a range of clinical entities, including skin and soft tissue infections, bacteremia, osteoarticular infections, and pleuropulmonary infections eg, pneumonia.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> remains at the forefront of infectious disease due to its production of protease, lipase, and hyaluronidase, as well as its ability to produce penicillin-binding protein 2A (PBP-2A) through the <italic toggle="yes">mecA</italic> gene, leading to the staphylococcal strain known as methicillin-resistant&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;(MRSA), which is resistant to beta-lactam antibiotics. Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29453"><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;Infections</ext-link>" and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/25074">Methicillin-Resistant</ext-link><italic toggle="yes"><ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/25074">&#x000a0;Staphylococcus aureus</ext-link>"</italic>&#x000a0;for further information</p>
        <p>Staphylococcal pneumonia accounts for approximately 2% to 3% of community-acquired pneumonia cases but is associated with significant morbidity due to its potential for rapid clinical deterioration. <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> is a common cause of hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia. Early recognition and prompt diagnosis are critical, as delayed treatment can lead to severe complications, including necrotizing pneumonia, bacteremia, and sepsis, which may occur with or without septic shock. This infection often develops following a recent viral illness, particularly influenza. Staphylococcal pneumonia should be considered in patients with specific risk factors, eg, recent hospitalization, prior antibiotic exposure, hemodialysis, or intravenous drug use.&#x000a0;The management of staphylococcal pneumonia&#x000a0;relies on interprofessional collaboration as coordinated, team-based care enhances clinical decision-making, supports timely interventions, and ultimately improves patient outcomes in this potentially life-threatening condition.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> infection represents an uncommon cause of community-acquired pneumonia, accounting for 1% to 3% of cases, yet it frequently occurs concurrently with or following viral infections, particularly influenza.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0; A bimodal distribution characterizes this infection, with increased risk observed in younger and older adults, while pneumococcus remains the leading cause of post-viral pneumonia in these groups.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Prior colonization with <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>, along with risk factors, eg, intravenous drug use, frequent hospitalizations, and recent antibiotic exposure, increases the likelihood of developing staphylococcal pneumonia. Bloodstream infection with <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> may progress to endocarditis, which can precede septic pulmonary emboli. Imaging findings may appear similar; however, the underlying mechanism differs, as septic emboli originate from a cardiac source rather than primary pulmonary pathology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>, particularly methicillin-resistant <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> (MRSA), serves as a relatively common cause of hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>When describing&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>, distinguishing MRSA from methicillin-susceptible&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;(MSSA) is crucial, as this will determine&#x000a0;treatment strategies for these patients. A large, international multicenter study of hospitalized patients with community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) found that 3% of patients developed CAP secondary to <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>. This had varying prevalence depending on the patient's place of residence. Approximately 51% of the&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>&#x000a0;isolates were found to be secondary to MRSA, and 49% were secondary to MSSA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Another study of inpatients hospitalized for CAP in the United States found that 1.7% were secondary to&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus,&#x000a0;</italic>of whom 0.7% were MRSA and 1% were MSSA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Although MRSA is an uncommon cause of CAP, vancomycin is frequently included in the empiric antibiotic regimen. To avoid unnecessary vancomycin use in this setting, a prospective, randomized study assessing the impact of nasal MRSA PCR testing on empiric antibiotic choice is currently underway.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>A large, prospective, multicenter study conducted in Europe&#x000a0;found that in patients requiring mechanical ventillation, the incidence of <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> intensive care unit pneumonia (SAIP) was 4.9 events per 1000 ICU-patient days. For patients colonized with <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>, the risk of SAIP was 3.6 times higher than in noncolonized patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;In contrast to the relatively uncommon&#x000a0;role of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>&#x000a0;in&#x000a0;CAP, MRSA is a relatively common cause of ventilator-associated pneumonia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Ventilator-associated pneumonia due to MRSA contributes to prolonged hospitalization and high mortality; however, this pathogen&#x000a0;can be difficult to diagnose in a timely fashion.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Bacteria and viruses are part of the healthy microbiome of the respiratory tract, with different organisms prevailing in different locations. Within the alveoli, anaerobic flora and microaerophilic streptococci predominate, and a mechanism of pneumonia development is the replication of these organisms. Another mechanism is a breakdown of the host's innate immune system, which can lead to an overwhelming proliferation of the normal, healthy lower respiratory tract flora.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Tobacco and alcohol use are known to often alter the host's defenses and predispose them to the development of pneumonia. More specifically, microaspiration of staphylococcal species from the nares can lead to staphylococcal pneumonia. Hematogenous spread from staphylococcal bacteremia and endocarditis can also develop into staphylococcal pneumonia.</p>
        <p>In postviral infections, studies have demonstrated increased <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> adhesion and protease production in the presence of influenza, leading to increased disease severity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Moreover, an in vitro study found that&#x000a0;concurrent influenza infection is permissive for the development of staphylococcal pneumonia caused by less virulent strains of the bacteria.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r13">[13]</xref> Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29453"><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;Infections</ext-link>" and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/25074">Methicillin-Resistant</ext-link><italic toggle="yes"><ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/25074">&#x000a0;Staphylococcus aureus</ext-link>"</italic> for further information on&#x000a0;the pathophysiology.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical History</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> infection can predispose patients to a wide range of infectious processes. Therefore, a thorough multisystem history and physical should be performed. When evaluating the need for hospitalization, care should be taken to assess the onset and duration of symptoms, as well as the presence of fever, chills, dyspnea, or productive cough. Approximately 80% of patients with pneumonia are febrile. However, this finding is less reliable in older adult patients.</p>
        <p>Evaluation of sick contacts, recent travel, and ongoing or recent influenza infection can also help&#x000a0;identify those at higher risk of developing&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S</italic><italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;aureus</italic>&#x000a0;pneumonia. Comorbid conditions, eg, hemodialysis, employment status, recent antibiotic use, and recent hospitalization, can increase suspicion for colonization with&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>.[CDC.&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.cdc.gov/staphylococcus-aureus/about/index.html"><italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>&#x000a0;Basics</ext-link>. 2024] History of&#x000a0;intravenous (IV) drug use should be ascertained, as tricuspid valve endocarditis from IV drug use can lead to septic emboli to the lungs and cause&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic>&#x000a0;pneumonia.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Physical Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>Physical exam should include a complete multisystem evaluation, with a focus on the cardiopulmonary examination to assess for signs of a valvular murmur, respiratory distress, tachypnea, or lobar-pattern inspiratory crackles. Percussion and tactile fremitus can also be utilized if a concern for pleural effusion is present. Unfortunately, no specific constellation of history and physical exam findings that reliably confirm a diagnosis of pneumonia has been established, but the previously discussed findings do support clinical suspicion for this disease process.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Initial evaluation for staphylococcal pneumonia begins with the same foundational assessment as for CAP of any etiology. A complete blood count (CBC) will likely show leukocytosis with a neutrophilic predominance. The gold standard for diagnosing pneumonia, with appropriate clinical suspicion, is the presence of an infiltrate on a chest radiograph.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;The infiltrate on a chest radiograph can be lobar or, in severe cases, cavitary lesions within the pulmonary parenchyma and empyema within the pleural space. If the clinical syndrome supports the diagnosis of pneumonia but the chest radiograph does not reveal evidence of disease, a computed tomography (CT) scan can be performed to further evaluate the lungs for pathology. For centers with appropriate expertise, lung ultrasound may also be used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Findings on a CT scan may show a developing lobar infiltrate or cavitary lesion that a chest radiograph may not reveal.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;A sputum sample can be obtained,&#x000a0;but&#x000a0;a 2005 study found that only 17% of the study population produced an adequate sample. However, if the patient can provide an adequate sample, 82% are diagnostic and can guide antibiotic therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;An MRSA swab may also be used to assess the patient's MRSA colonization status and, if negative, has shown a negative predictive value of 96.1% to 99.2% for MRSA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;However, an MRSA-positive nasopharyngeal swab has a poor positive predictive value.</p>
        <p>Obtaining lower respiratory tract specimens remains a key factor in establishing the diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;A procalcitonin level can also be drawn at the time of treatment to guide antibiotic selection and support antibiotic stewardship.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;Thus, the diagnosis of <italic toggle="yes">S aureus</italic> pneumonia typically requires a combination of clinical, radiographic, and laboratory findings. The latter may include respiratory specimens with a predominance of gram-positive cocci in clusters, respiratory cultures, and blood cultures that grow <italic toggle="yes">S&#x000a0;aureus.</italic>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Treatment for staphylococcal pneumonia depends on whether the infection is MRSA or MSSA. If MRSA is suspected or confirmed, either vancomycin or linezolid should be initiated at the start of treatment and promptly discontinued if MRSA is ruled out. Both vancomycin and linezolid have similar efficacy, and selection should be based on patient tolerance, antibiotic allergy profile, renal function, drug interaction, and IV access. Because linezolid is systemically bioavailable when administered orally, this route is preferred if IV access is unavailable.</p>
        <p>Vancomycin is preferred if the patient is cytopenic or taking selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Specifically for MRSA, recent guidelines no longer recommend monitoring vancomycin trough concentrations due to limited data on its efficacy and the potential toxicity associated with this target. Current recommendations are for an AUC/MIC ratio of 400 to 600 (by broth microdilution).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30965">Vancomycin</ext-link>" and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/24291">Linezolid</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Ceftaroline can be used if vancomycin or linezolid are contraindicated; however, this agent is not FDA-approved for hospital-acquired pneumonia or ventilator-associated pneumonia and has broader gram-negative coverage, making&#x000a0;ceftaroline less ideal from an antimicrobial stewardship standpoint. Clindamycin is also an alternative, but this agent is less efficacious for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Studies&#x000a0;are underway to assess the role of nasal MRSA PCR swab testing in identifying patients with CAP who do not require empiric vancomycin therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Duration of treatment varies depending on complicating factors, eg, empyema, endocarditis, and bloodstream infection. For&#x000a0;CAP without increased risk factors, a duration of at least 5 days has been recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>If culture results grow MSSA and rule out other causes of pneumonia, then therapy can be de-escalated to nafcillin, oxacillin, or cefazolin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;Supportive measures for staphylococcal pneumonia include bronchodilation to help those with underlying lung diseases, eg, asthma or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Supplemental oxygenation may also be needed due to restrictive lung disease from the pneumonia process, and can begin with a nasal cannula. However, if the disease process is severe enough, then mechanical ventilation may be necessary.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>As the clinical presentation for pneumonia and, therefore, staphylococcal pneumonia is broad and nonspecific, so is the differential for this process; therefore, differential diagnoses that should also be considered include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pneumonia secondary to:</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Streptococcal pneumonia&#x000a0;</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Nontypeable <italic toggle="yes">Haemophilus influenza</italic>&#x000a0;</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p><italic toggle="yes">Mycoplasma</italic> pneumonia&#x000a0;</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p><italic toggle="yes">Chlamydia</italic> pneumonia&#x000a0;</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p><italic toggle="yes">Legionella</italic>&#x000a0;</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Gram-negative bacilli&#x000a0;</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Group A <italic toggle="yes">Streptococcus</italic>&#x000a0;</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Anaerobic infection&#x000a0;</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p><italic toggle="yes">Mycoplasma tuberculosis</italic>&#x000a0;</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Viral infection&#x000a0;</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pulmonary edema secondary to congestive heart failure&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pulmonary hemorrhage&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Atelectasis&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pneumonitis from aspiration or chemical exposure&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Interstitial lung disease&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Drug reaction&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pulmonary embolism</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lung Cancer</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s10" sec-type="Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials">
        <title>Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials</title>
        <p>A current study protocol for a randomized controlled trial of staphylococcal pneumonia is the Swab Testing to Optimize Pneumonia treatment with empiric Vancomycin (STOP-Vanc).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of staphylococcal pneumonia is highly dependent on the patient's comorbid conditions, underlying lung disease, and sequelae of the disease process. The presence of bacteremia or empyema can worsen prognosis, further underscoring the importance of astute clinical suspicion and appropriate antibiotic intervention.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>A significant complication of staphylococcal pneumonia is the development of severe necrotizing pneumonia. This can be seen in community-associated-MRSA (CA-MRSA).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;If this is clinically concerning, a chest CT may be warranted to further assess for this pathology. Notably, a chest CT should not be used for the initial diagnosis of pneumonia; a chest x-ray should be ordered first as a foundational part of the initial evaluation. Staphylococcal pneumonia may also lead to complications, eg, sepsis, septic shock, bacteremia, respiratory failure leading to invasive mechanical ventilation, and even death.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patients should be educated about the presentation of CAP in the outpatient setting, as this could lead to faster diagnosis and, in appropriate clinical scenarios, to the possibility of staphylococcal pneumonia. Furthermore, patient education is important, as prompt antibiotic therapy can help resolve this infectious process before sequelae, eg, sepsis, bacteremia, or respiratory failure, occur. People should be educated about the risks of influenza, most notably its association with severe staphylococcal pneumonia. Additionally, patients should be encouraged to receive influenza vaccines.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29450.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Staphylococcal pneumonia, caused by <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>, represents an uncommon but clinically significant cause of community-acquired pneumonia and a frequent etiology of hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia. Disease severity stems from virulence factors and, in methicillin-resistant strains, resistance mediated by the <italic toggle="yes">mecA</italic> gene. Infection often follows viral illness, particularly influenza, with enhanced bacterial adhesion and toxin production contributing to rapid progression. Clinical presentation includes fever, dyspnea, and productive cough, with potential for necrotizing pneumonia, bacteremia, and sepsis. Diagnosis relies on clinical suspicion, supported by imaging findings of infiltrates or cavitary lesions, and microbiologic confirmation from respiratory and blood cultures. Management requires prompt initiation of empiric therapy targeting MRSA when risk factors exist, followed by de-escalation based on culture results, alongside supportive care and monitoring for complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29450.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration is essential to optimize outcomes and reduce morbidity. Physicians and advanced practitioners lead diagnostic evaluation and therapeutic decision-making, while primary care clinicians contribute to early recognition, risk assessment, and post-discharge follow-up. Nurses play a critical role in monitoring clinical status, administering therapies, and identifying early deterioration. Pharmacists guide antimicrobial selection, dosing, and stewardship, including therapeutic monitoring for agents such as vancomycin. Respiratory therapists support oxygenation and ventilation strategies, while case managers and social workers facilitate care transitions and adherence. Effective communication, shared decision-making, and coordinated care pathways promote timely escalation, appropriate referral, and prevention of complications, ultimately improving patient safety and quality of care.</p>
      </sec>
